"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[99328],{899328:e=>{e.exports="# Functions & Stored Procedures\n\nSource dialect stored procedure / UDF syntax → DBSQL equivalents. Includes cursors literal translation.\n\n> For DBSQL stored procedure syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/stored-procedures.md`.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### Stored Procedures\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nCREATE PROCEDURE dbo.MyProc\n    @param1 INT,\n    @param2 VARCHAR(50) = 'default',\n    @output INT OUTPUT\nAS BEGIN\n    SET @output = @param1 * 2;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL (Unity Catalog three-part name: catalog.schema.procedure)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.my_proc(\n    IN param1 INT,\n    IN param2 STRING DEFAULT 'default',\n    OUT output_param INT\n)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nAS BEGIN\n    SET output_param = param1 * 2;\nEND;\n```\n\n**Security context (`SQL SECURITY INVOKER`)**: Always include `SQL SECURITY INVOKER` — it is a required clause and the only accepted value in Databricks SQL (`SQL SECURITY DEFINER` is not valid). Omitting it causes `MISSING_CLAUSES_FOR_OPERATION: SQL SECURITY` (SQLSTATE 42601). The procedure runs with the caller's permissions. Note: this differs from T-SQL's default owner-chain permission model — if the original procedure relied on owner-chain access to objects the caller cannot see directly, flag this in a migration comment.\n\nCalling a procedure with OUT parameter:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nEXEC dbo.MyProc @param1 = 10, @output = @result OUTPUT;\n\n-- DBSQL (session-level call from a notebook cell)\nDECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE result INT;\nCALL my_catalog.my_schema.my_proc(10, 'default', result);\n```\n\n**BEGIN vs BEGIN ATOMIC — choose based on T-SQL transaction intent:**\n\nAnalyze the T-SQL source to determine transaction intent, then map:\n\n| T-SQL pattern                                                           | Databricks                           | Why                                                      |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| No `BEGIN TRANSACTION` (autocommit)                                     | `BEGIN...END`                        | Semantically equivalent — per-statement autocommit       |\n| `BEGIN TRANSACTION` + `COMMIT` + `TRY/CATCH` with `ROLLBACK`            | `BEGIN ATOMIC`                       | Automatic all-or-nothing without manual transaction code |\n| Contains DDL (`CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`)                                | `BEGIN...END`                        | DDL not allowed in atomic blocks                         |\n| Custom error handling (logging, partial recovery, conditional rollback) | `BEGIN...END` with `DECLARE HANDLER` | Atomic blocks don't allow exception handlers             |\n\n**How to identify transaction intent in T-SQL:**\n\n- Explicit `BEGIN TRANSACTION` / `BEGIN TRAN` → procedure wants atomicity\n- `TRY/CATCH` with `ROLLBACK` in the catch → confirms rollback-on-error intent\n- `SET XACT_ABORT ON` at procedure start → errors should abort the transaction\n- None of the above → procedure relies on autocommit (no atomicity needed)\n\n**Always add a comment explaining the mapping:**\n\n```sql\n-- Mapped to BEGIN ATOMIC: T-SQL used BEGIN TRANSACTION with TRY/CATCH rollback\n-- Mapped to BEGIN: T-SQL relied on autocommit (no explicit transaction)\n-- Mapped to BEGIN: T-SQL contains DDL / requires exception handlers\n```\n\n```sql\n-- DML-only procedure (no RETURNS TABLE)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.proc_name(IN param1 INT)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    INSERT INTO t SELECT param1;\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'done' WHERE id = param1;\nEND;\n\n-- Procedure that returns a result set (RETURNS TABLE)\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.proc_name(IN param1 INT)\nRETURNS TABLE (col1 INT, col2 STRING)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    SELECT col1, col2 FROM t WHERE id = param1;\nEND;\n```\n\n**`RETURNS TABLE` requires `BEGIN ATOMIC`**: Procedures with an explicit return schema (`RETURNS TABLE (col1 TYPE, ...)`) must use `BEGIN ATOMIC`. This is a language requirement — the procedure guarantees a specific result shape, which requires transactional semantics. Bare `BEGIN` with `RETURNS TABLE` is a syntax error.\n\n**Multi-result-set mapping:**\n\nDatabricks SQL procedures surface only the **last** SELECT statement's result to the caller. T-SQL procedures that return multiple result sets must be restructured during conversion — never leave multiple bare SELECTs expecting all to be returned. The translation changes the caller contract, so **every multi-result-set procedure requires a `-- TODO: manual review required — <reason>` comment** on the converted procedure regardless of which option below you pick; downstream code consuming the procedure will need to be checked.\n\n- **Option A (preferred when schemas are compatible)**: Combine with `UNION ALL` and a discriminator column. The discriminator column is not in the original output schema.\n  ```sql\n  -- TODO: manual review required — T-SQL returned 2 result sets. Combined with UNION ALL + result_set discriminator.\n  -- The discriminator column was added and is not in the original schema; callers indexing specific columns need updates.\n  SELECT 'set1' AS result_set, col1, col2 FROM t1\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT 'set2' AS result_set, col1, col2 FROM t2\n  ```\n- **Option B**: Restructure to a single final SELECT, staging intermediate results in temp tables or CTEs. Only the final SELECT returns to the caller.\n- **Option C (last resort — incompatible schemas only)**: When result sets cannot be combined due to incompatible column counts or types, do not apply autonomously — surface as a blocker with the proposed discard strategy in the `suggestion` field (which set to keep, which to drop, and why A and B don't work). On re-invocation after the caller confirms, select the authoritative result set and document what was discarded:\n  ```sql\n  -- NOTE: T-SQL returned N result sets. Databricks SQL procedures return only the last\n  -- SELECT result. Returning <which set and why>. Discarded sets: <list>.\n  -- If the application depends on discarded result sets, a refactor is required.\n  ```\n\n### User-Defined Functions (UDFs)\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL scalar UDF\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.GetFullName(@first VARCHAR(50), @last VARCHAR(50))\nRETURNS VARCHAR(100)\nAS BEGIN\n    RETURN @first + ' ' + @last;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL (Unity Catalog three-part name: catalog.schema.function)\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.get_full_name(first STRING, last STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nRETURN CONCAT(first, ' ', last);\n```\n\nTable-valued functions (TVFs): Inline single-SELECT TVFs have a direct DBSQL equivalent (`RETURNS TABLE ... RETURN SELECT ...`). Multi-statement TVFs (with procedural logic) have no direct equivalent — convert to a **Python UDF returning a table** if parameters are needed, or a non-parameterized view if the TVF takes no arguments. Do not convert to a plain SQL view when the TVF accepts parameters — views are not parameterized.\n\n### Cursors (literal translation)\n\n**Prefer set-based rewrites** (see `tsql/02-select-dml.md`). Use literal translation only when row-by-row semantics are strictly required. Cursor syntax requires **DBR 18.1+ / SQL Serverless**. Not supported in notebooks or jobs running on classic or serverless compute clusters.\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL cursor syntax (Serverless SQL)\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE v_id INT;\n    DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;\n    DECLARE cur CURSOR FOR SELECT id FROM source_table;\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n\n    OPEN cur;\n    fetch_loop: LOOP\n        FETCH cur INTO v_id;\n        IF done = 1 THEN LEAVE fetch_loop; END IF;\n        UPDATE other_table SET processed = 1 WHERE id = v_id;\n    END LOOP fetch_loop;\n    CLOSE cur;\nEND;\n```\n\n> The cursor syntax above is complete. Load `skills/writing-sql/sql-scripting.md` for general scripting constructs (variables, IF/ELSE, WHILE, error handlers) — it does not document cursor syntax.\n\n### Error Handling (TRY/CATCH → DECLARE HANDLER)\n\nT-SQL uses `BEGIN TRY / BEGIN CATCH` blocks. DBSQL uses `DECLARE ... HANDLER` inside a `BEGIN ... END` block. Handlers must be declared before any executable statements in the block.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nBEGIN TRY\n    INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\n    DECLARE @msg NVARCHAR(4000) = ERROR_MESSAGE();\n    RAISERROR(@msg, 16, 1);\nEND CATCH;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n    BEGIN\n        -- SQLSTATE and MESSAGE_TEXT are available in handler scope\n        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Insert failed';\n    END;\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Key differences:**\n\n| T-SQL                                             | DBSQL                                                 | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `BEGIN TRY ... END TRY BEGIN CATCH ... END CATCH` | `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION BEGIN ... END` | Handler declared at top of block; `EXIT` means exit the block on error                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `RAISERROR(msg, severity, state)`                 | `SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'msg'`    | Use `'45000'` for user-defined errors; SQLSTATE `'45000'` is the generic unhandled exception state                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `THROW` (re-throw)                                | `RESIGNAL`                                            | Re-raises the current exception inside a handler                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `ERROR_MESSAGE()`                                 | `MESSAGE_TEXT` condition information item             | Available inside the handler scope                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `ERROR_NUMBER()`                                  | No equivalent                                         | `MYSQL_ERRNO` is **not available** in Databricks SQL. Use literal `-1` or `NULL` with a `-- ERROR_NUMBER() not supported` comment.                                                                                                                      |\n| `ERROR_STATE()`                                   | `RETURNED_SQLSTATE` (partial)                         | `GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 s = RETURNED_SQLSTATE` returns a 5-char SQLSTATE string (e.g. `'22012'`), not a numeric state code. Use as the closest available equivalent; if schema compatibility requires an integer, use literal `-1` with a comment. |\n| `@@ERROR`                                         | `SQLSTATE`                                            | Available immediately after a statement; covered in `00-quirks.md`                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n\n**CONTINUE vs EXIT handlers:**\n\n- `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER` — exits the block when triggered (equivalent to T-SQL CATCH which exits the TRY block)\n- `DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER` — resumes execution after the failing statement (no T-SQL equivalent; T-SQL always exits to CATCH)\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL: EXIT handler (most common T-SQL equivalent)\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Transaction failed';\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL: CONTINUE handler (suppress errors and keep going — no T-SQL equivalent)\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n    -- Used in cursor loops (see cursor section above)\nEND;\n```\n\n### Variables\n\n**`SET` syntax — procedure-local vs. session level (critical distinction):**\n\n| Context                                 | Declare                                             | Assign                |\n| --------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| Inside `BEGIN...END` / stored procedure | `DECLARE var TYPE DEFAULT val;`                     | `SET var = expr;`     |\n| Session level (outside any block)       | `DECLARE OR REPLACE VARIABLE var TYPE DEFAULT val;` | `SET VAR var = expr;` |\n\n**Inside a procedure body, always use `SET var = expr` — NOT `SET VAR var = expr`.** The `SET VAR` form is for session-level variables only; using it inside a `BEGIN...END` block causes a `PARSE_SYNTAX_ERROR`.\n\nEvery variable used with `SET` inside a block must first be declared with `DECLARE` in the same block (or an enclosing block). You cannot `SET` a variable that has not been declared.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nDECLARE @var INT = 10;\nSET @var = (SELECT MAX(id) FROM t);\nSELECT @a = col1, @b = col2 FROM t WHERE id = 1;\n\n-- DBSQL (inside procedure or compound statement)\nDECLARE var INT DEFAULT 10;\nSET var = (SELECT MAX(id) FROM t);\nSET (a, b) = (SELECT col1, col2 FROM t WHERE id = 1);\n```\n\n**`DECLARE` placement — must be inside `BEGIN...END`, at the top of the block**: `DECLARE` statements must appear inside `BEGIN...END`, not before it. Placing `DECLARE` outside the procedure body causes `PARSE_SYNTAX_ERROR: extra input '<variable_name>'`. Strip the `@` prefix from T-SQL variable names; the rest of the name is kept as-is:\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL stored procedure with local variables\nCREATE PROCEDURE dbo.MyProc(@p1 INT)\nAS BEGIN\n    DECLARE @v_name VARCHAR(100);\n    DECLARE @count INT = 0;\n    SET @v_name = 'value';\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL: DECLARE goes inside BEGIN at the top, before any executable statements\nCREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE my_catalog.my_schema.my_proc(IN p1 INT)\nLANGUAGE SQL\nSQL SECURITY INVOKER\nAS BEGIN\n    DECLARE v_name STRING;          -- @v_name VARCHAR(100) → v_name STRING\n    DECLARE count INT DEFAULT 0;    -- @count INT = 0 → count INT DEFAULT 0\n    SET v_name = 'value';           -- SET (no VAR) inside a procedure body\nEND;\n```\n\n**Variable assignment edge cases:**\n\n- If the `SELECT` returns **0 rows**, the variable retains its declared default value (same as T-SQL behavior with `SELECT @var = col`).\n- If the `SELECT` returns **multiple rows**, DBSQL raises an error. In T-SQL, `SELECT @var = col` silently takes the last row's value — this is a behavioral change. If the source relies on the last-row behavior, rewrite as `SELECT MAX(col)` or add a `LIMIT 1` with an explicit `ORDER BY`.\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL. Note: SYNAPSE does not support stored procedures with OUTPUT parameters in external pools — check compute target before migrating.\n"}}]);
